Author:     totalwindupflightsystems <totalwindupflightsystems@gmail.com>
AuthorDate: Fri Oct 23 13:54:59 2026 -0500
Commit:     totalwindupflightsystems <totalwindupflightsystems@gmail.com>
CommitDate: Fri Oct 23 13:54:59 2026 -0500

    perf(seed): batch corpus writes to reduce fsync overhead. Addresses PERF-OFF-BY-ONE-1.
    
    The seed loader autocommitted every statement, so a fresh install paid
    one fsync per row in WAL mode: ~9,100 fsyncs, 45-70s wall on ext4 for
    ~4s of work (tmpfs control). The load now runs in two phases: read and
    decode every corpus file into memory first (filesystem I/O stays outside
    the write transaction), then write all classes/answers in ONE graph.Tx —
    a new batched-writes type whose methods run the same function bodies as
    the Store counterparts over a queryer executor, so validation,
    normalization (REVIEW-OB-010) and dedup cannot drift. The linker keeps
    its own post-commit batch transaction, unchanged.
    
    Semantics preserved: same Seed signature, same load order (sorted
    filenames), same upsert/dedup/status/signature mapping, same idempotent
    re-runs. The corpus load is now atomic: a write error or a cancelled
    context rolls the batch back and the idempotent loader re-imports
    everything on the next run.
    
    Measured on ext4 (fresh DB, corpus 2860 files): 4.17s wall (was
    44.70-70.51s), 29 fsyncs under strace (was 9,136); tmpfs control 3.74s.
    Output unchanged: fresh seed classes=2860 created / 0 existing,
    answers=2959 created / 1530 skipped, edges=12890 created; re-run
    classes=0 created / 2860 existing, answers=0 created / 4489 skipped,
    edges=0.
    
    Co-authored-by: Alexis Okuwa <wojonstech@gmail.com>
